[{:db/id 17592186221401 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" "question-34486fa6-cccf-5fd8-a975-d4b80d4ead78" "question-a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" "question-ba7a8267-d75d-5d4b-bc96-5af23d284353"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221184 :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $\\Delta x$ is\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = 4(\\Delta x)^3 + (\\Delta x)^4.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.4,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/d6/d6591c7db007621e1868ad178b09b55025845f041d952272c098635c76d54a83.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ With step size $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the global truncation error is ![](images/27/2760e0882373e1a374fa21f99b1bb5055e7dd76a64812c2a7a0d340678506433.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ Decreasing from $\\Delta x = 0.4$ to $\\Delta x = 0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of ![](images/2e/2e3028c5348dcee8040ea67df7f90b2a43bbac8c69774981b978ac5dfb2150b0.svg)\n\n$\\quad$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n![](images/f8/f81c63d72e983755776cec5ce37e32be9ba8b9c331bc793434745502596e5886.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d97bcc9d-8c7b-5d3a-9dd6-550b8b7b1d07" "question-1812d74c-f7ff-5b92-ad0c-d300745fe3f0" "question-a4879a58-4027-569c-b7a3-4f905f12c0fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99c7bcc8-5821-518d-9fe0-5a2eb829c883" "question-dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" "question-2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221186 :question/problem "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\\tau$) represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(\\Delta x) = 3(\\Delta x)^2 + 4(\\Delta x)^5$\n\nII. $\\tau(\\Delta x) = 0.6(\\Delta x)^3 + 2(\\Delta x)^4$\n\nIII. $e(\\Delta x) = 7(\\Delta x)^4 + 5(\\Delta x)^6$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf397494-3423-5d84-aaa6-feb204494ce6" "question-42270ee1-30c3-599c-b59e-da86dc2e3b89"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221187 :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$\nSuppose that using step size $\\Delta x = 0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $\\tau(0.4) \\approx 0.24.$\nIf we instead use a step size $\\Delta x = 0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n![](images/5a/5a748823d0f54b92891d55761d2cecdd788bf797dd313173315e7731b6f4d0bc.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" :question/id #uuid "ec94fc3e-e8fc-535e-a62b-74b6f854910e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334564" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{3}-(0.6)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.216-1\\cdot 0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.296-0.129\\,6 \\\\  & =1.166\\,4 \\\\  & ≈1.166.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.3) & =6(0.3)^{3}-(0.3)^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.027-1\\cdot 0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.162-0.008\\,1 \\\\  & =0.153\\,9 \\\\  & ≈0.154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.3.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.3}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.166\\,4$ to $0.153\\,9.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.166\\,4}{0.153\\,9}=7.578\\,94…≈7.579.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈7.578\\,94… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(7.578\\,94…) \\\\ p & ≈2.922….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{3})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7959fd50-2a91-5106-ad86-f9bd30e714cd" "answer-field-db9d26b2-c636-50cd-b971-f41c9e5386dd" "answer-field-22224acc-e8fd-5f6b-987d-4bc060c0fd71" "answer-field-b7969c52-bb2d-55c2-ad55-3063b09bd2c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7959fd50-2a91-5106-ad86-f9bd30e714cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "7959fd50-2a91-5106-ad86-f9bd30e714cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e1d19942-8bc6-564c-8a4b-4b40ad17ff5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-474297a2-e972-57c3-9d68-3d32357d4677" "answer-5228ef68-3438-5213-92b0-4e0d6