lidate]} {:db/id "answer-ed993786-8a89-51cf-bcfa-dc0e023abeb3" :answer/id #uuid "ed993786-8a89-51cf-bcfa-dc0e023abeb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.004" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7969c52-bb2d-55c2-ad55-3063b09bd2c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7969c52-bb2d-55c2-ad55-3063b09bd2c2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-68bf3506-76d5-5c52-9134-2b2746381b6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5472a55e-6216-537d-be5c-d2f3e7bed487" "answer-f6a3bcc0-0837-5f72-95fd-f73b9e66c4c0" "answer-68bf3506-76d5-5c52-9134-2b2746381b6d" "answer-f790d854-d14d-5074-bad9-4ec3940b30e0" "answer-8ab93fca-29ed-5b1f-acff-3ede7d116441"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5472a55e-6216-537d-be5c-d2f3e7bed487" :answer/id #uuid "5472a55e-6216-537d-be5c-d2f3e7bed487" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6a3bcc0-0837-5f72-95fd-f73b9e66c4c0" :answer/id #uuid "f6a3bcc0-0837-5f72-95fd-f73b9e66c4c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68bf3506-76d5-5c52-9134-2b2746381b6d" :answer/id #uuid "68bf3506-76d5-5c52-9134-2b2746381b6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f790d854-d14d-5074-bad9-4ec3940b30e0" :answer/id #uuid "f790d854-d14d-5074-bad9-4ec3940b30e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ab93fca-29ed-5b1f-acff-3ede7d116441" :answer/id #uuid "8ab93fca-29ed-5b1f-acff-3ede7d116441" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34486fa6-cccf-5fd8-a975-d4b80d4ead78" :question/id #uuid "34486fa6-cccf-5fd8-a975-d4b80d4ead78" :question/math-academy-id "q-334545" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.410.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.026.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.410$ to $0.026.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.410}{0.026}=15.769….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈15.769… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(15.769…) \\\\ p & ≈3.979….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-363cd73d-f05d-5d69-8b42-f72895d3771c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-363cd73d-f05d-5d69-8b42-f72895d3771c" :answer-field/id #uuid "363cd73d-f05d-5d69-8b42-f72895d3771c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51897522-282c-5afb-8a2d-f610a69ae795" "answer-b1090be2-8599-5d76-a4a0-6edb0f56303b" "answer-fd8d57dc-394d-5daa-9001-0b4b29133d6a" "answer-29061ed8-03ee-5954-99b1-36ee8a7d846e" "answer-5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51897522-282c-5afb-8a2d-f610a69ae795" :answer/id #uuid "51897522-282c-5afb-8a2d-f610a69ae795" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1090be2-8599-5d76-a4a0-6edb0f56303b" :answer/id #uuid "b1090be2-8599-5d76-a4a0-6edb0f56303b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd8d57dc-394d-5daa-9001-0b4b29133d6a" :answer/id #uuid "fd8d57dc-394d-5daa-9001-0b4b29133d6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29061ed8-03ee-5954-99b1-36ee8a7d846e" :answer/id #uuid "29061ed8-03ee-5954-99b1-36ee8a7d846e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f" :answer/id #uuid "5751dcbe-8a8b-5270-9916-49268913535f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" :question/id #uuid "a67eecd4-7182-53b5-9971-e13bae152b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-334561" :question/problem "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.4$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =5(0.4)^{4}+(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0256+1\\cdot 0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.128+0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.138\\,24 \\\\  & ≈0.138.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}+(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0016+1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008+0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008\\,32 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.138\\,24$ to $0.008\\,32.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.138\\,24}{0.008\\,32}=16.615\\,384…≈16.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈16.615\\,384… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(16.615\\,384…) \\\\ p & ≈4.054….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a088a7b0-d0de-5845-8835-029386c41403" "answer-field-4c7e74ee-940c-59df-8849-80e54b1b5cba" "answer-field-e8d5da60-b415-58cd-91b5-f58b2bd119a3" "answer-field-251effb9-9c02-568e-85ed-f4b7bd54ba26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a088a7b0-d0de-5845-8835-029386c41403" :answer-field/id #uuid "a088a7b0-d