60" :answer/id #uuid "cd3222b9-81c4-5af0-93b8-c8fc2af4c060" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c212b18-052b-5947-83ed-2750ffa5a330" :answer/id #uuid "7c212b18-052b-5947-83ed-2750ffa5a330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cf9cf30-7ba4-5601-a3c0-9a8f173d1297" :answer/id #uuid "7cf9cf30-7ba4-5601-a3c0-9a8f173d1297" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-99c7bcc8-5821-518d-9fe0-5a2eb829c883" :question/id #uuid "99c7bcc8-5821-518d-9fe0-5a2eb829c883" :question/math-academy-id "q-334746" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{3}+(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+9(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc230a0b-2414-5af0-ae24-e7a4a94012f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc230a0b-2414-5af0-ae24-e7a4a94012f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc230a0b-2414-5af0-ae24-e7a4a94012f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c6e1ed0-06b1-5ca3-ad4a-39554d1e3182" "answer-60dcb97a-6c3e-59a6-ab57-540ba0016358" "answer-800f335d-43f8-577e-9911-2a7ac4b4cfc0" "answer-fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" "answer-445b4972-2bf0-5719-9a37-bca571bea627"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c6e1ed0-06b1-5ca3-ad4a-39554d1e3182" :answer/id #uuid "5c6e1ed0-06b1-5ca3-ad4a-39554d1e3182" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60dcb97a-6c3e-59a6-ab57-540ba0016358" :answer/id #uuid "60dcb97a-6c3e-59a6-ab57-540ba0016358" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-800f335d-43f8-577e-9911-2a7ac4b4cfc0" :answer/id #uuid "800f335d-43f8-577e-9911-2a7ac4b4cfc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" :answer/id #uuid "fd06ae41-23c0-58e5-92fd-7205a48af613" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-445b4972-2bf0-5719-9a37-bca571bea627" :answer/id #uuid "445b4972-2bf0-5719-9a37-bca571bea627" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221982 :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" :question/id #uuid "dd55dc02-570c-5acd-bec7-3aaa43923830" :question/math-academy-id "q-334750" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{2}=0.4(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{2}+9(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{2}=5(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e723f15c-67d1-5701-9063-4001a95d8429"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e723f15c-67d1-5701-9063-4001a95d8429" :answer-field/id #uuid "e723f15c-67d1-5701-9063-4001a95d8429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df73a5d1-7721-5994-9aa6-d617ef112c6d" "answer-418b4194-ffcb-5bd9-80c5-0907e534d558" "answer-d35050c1-1a71-58b1-9e7e-7ad043e59fe6" "answer-51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" "answer-a3c5ba70-5266-5bbc-9058-2e20c6deb40b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df73a5d1-7721-5994-9aa6-d617ef112c6d" :answer/id #uuid "df73a5d1-7721-5994-9aa6-d617ef112c6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-418b4194-ffcb-5bd9-80c5-0907e534d558" :answer/id #uuid "418b4194-ffcb-5bd9-80c5-0907e534d558" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d35050c1-1a71-58b1-9e7e-7ad043e59fe6" :answer/id #uuid "d35050c1-1a71-58b1-9e7e-7ad043e59fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" :answer/id #uuid "51d5c0c3-9d4b-5c33-ba7e-37ba2404b976" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3c5ba70-5266-5bbc-9058-2e20c6deb40b" :answer/id #uuid "a3c5ba70-5266-5bbc-9058-2e20c6deb40b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550" :question/id #uuid "2d0aee14-de07-5ea6-8cb4-30e573b28550" :question/math-academy-id "q-334739" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.4Δx+5(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4Δx.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=7(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{2}+0.2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8840ee7-0877-5782-9c59-9fed15f7a713"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8840ee7-0877-5782-9c59-9fed15f7a713" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8840ee7-0877-5782-9c59-9fed15f7a713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f412d79a-33f6-5bcb-b658-0b1fc288cc46" "answer-218a2453-01ac-5477-9e84-010ad313a737" "answer-f115cc39-13ba-5329-863a-fd3838a1bef8" "answer-5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" "answer-6ebae68b-3031-59b4-86e5-efe42825b7a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f412d79a-33f6-5bcb-b658-0b1fc288cc46" :answer/id #uuid "f412d79a-33f6-5bcb-b658-0b1fc288cc46" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-218a2453-01ac-5477-9e84-010ad313a737" :answer/id #uuid "218a2453-01ac-5477-9e84-010ad313a737" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f115cc39-13ba-5329-863a-fd3838a1bef8" :answer/id #uuid "f115cc39-13ba-5329-863a-fd3838a1bef8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" :answer/id #uuid "5869c9cf-72ad-57b8-9b01-afed018068ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ebae68b-3031-59b4-86e5-efe42825b7a7" :answer/id #uuid "6ebae68b-3031-59b4-86e5-efe42825b7a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308676 :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{5}+0.5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf397494-3423-5d84-aaa6-feb204494ce6" :question/id #uuid "cf397494-3423-5d84-aaa6-feb204494ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334953" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.27.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={3}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.27}{9}=0.03.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b8f920a9-df32-5f82-a2de-198e71a776e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8f920a9-df32-5f82-a2de-198e71a776e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f920a9-df32-5f82-a2de-198e71a776e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151" :answer/id #uuid "5a8847bb-f2df-592f-a626-453571669151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.03" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221970 :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.072.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.05,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.05)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.05,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.05)≈\\frac{0.072}{4}=0.018.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220456 :answer-field/correct "answer-cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937" :answer/id #uuid "cb49b900-5044-5955-8823-d0d1314a5937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.018" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-42270ee1-30c3-599c-b59e-da86dc2e3b89" :question/id #uuid "42270ee1-30c3-599c-b59e-da86dc2e3b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-334952" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈1.215.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={3}^{5}=243.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.215}{243}=0.005.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-acf8e5b1-b98e-51b0-8a06-878baca2b0a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-acf8e5b1-b98e-51b0-8a06-878baca2b0a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "acf8e5b1-b98e-51b0-8a06-878baca2b0a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" :answer/id #uuid "3c888821-4abd-556d-946d-6732bb9e8b80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.005" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222498 :tutorial/content "Each step of a numerical method produces a local truncation error $\\tau,$ which, over multiple steps, accumulates into a global truncation error $e.$ Both LTE and GTE depend on step size $\\Delta x.$\n\nThe **order** of a numerical method describes how fast these errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\nA numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the *largest positive* integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(\\frac{Δx}{k}) & ≈K{(\\frac{Δx}{k})}^{p}=\\frac{K(Δx)^{p}}{{k}^{p}}≈\\frac{e(Δx)}{{k}^{p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate with an example. Suppose a numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.21,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.21) = 0.785.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.07,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.07) = 0.010.$\n\nUsing this data, we can estimate the order of the numerical method.\n\nThe step size decreases from $0.21$ to $0.07.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.21}{0.07} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 3^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.785$ to $0.010.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.785}{0.010} = 78.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈78.5 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(78.5) \\\\ p & ≈3.97….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$\n\nBut why is order important? Well, a higher order means that the error decreases faster as $\\Delta x$ decreases. As a result, higher-order methods can achieve a target accuracy *with fewer steps*. In this way, order predicts the accuracy you gain when you make steps smaller." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221478 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
